policy_module(udevkobjectuevent, 1.0.0)

# Evidenced by round 5 of tmt/mls-hang-live-ssh-real.fmf (t=5.284s in the
# newly-captured early-boot window, journalctl -k source):
#
#   avc: denied { read write } for pid=622 comm="systemd-udevd"
#     path="socket:[3414]" dev="sockfs" ino=3414
#     scontext=system_u:system_r:udev_t:s0-s15:c0.c1023
#     tcontext=system_u:system_r:kernel_t:s15:c0.c1023
#     tclass=netlink_kobject_uevent_socket permissive=0
#
# This is NOT the earlier already-known auditd.conf-style MLS level/range
# mismatch: policy/mls explicitly documents netlink_kobject_uevent_socket
# as having "no MLS restrictions" (see the commented-out class list next to
# the socket-class mlsconstrain block), and none of the mlsconstrain
# statements for socket classes actually enumerate this class -- confirmed
# by reading policy/mls directly. So this denial cannot be an MLS
# range/level problem; it is a genuine missing TE allow rule.
#
# Confirmed by grepping the *entire* reference policy tree
# (policy/modules/**/*.te, *.if): every single existing grant of
# netlink_kobject_uevent_socket access, for every domain in the tree
# (udev_t itself included, policy/modules/system/udev.te:57), is a
# "self:netlink_kobject_uevent_socket" rule -- i.e. a domain accessing a
# socket labeled with its OWN type. No domain anywhere is granted access to
# a netlink_kobject_uevent_socket labeled kernel_t. That is exactly the
# combination denied here: udev_t (scontext) against a socket labeled
# kernel_t (tcontext), not against itself.
#
# The syscall audit record sharing this event's serial number is an
# execve() of /usr/lib/systemd/systemd-udevd (success, exit=0) -- i.e. this
# fires as an inherited-fd re-check at exec time, not at socket(2)/bind(2)
# time. The self: rule already in udev.te covers udevd's own
# freshly-created uevent socket; this additional, narrower rule is only for
# the specific kernel_t-labeled instance actually observed (most plausibly
# a socket fd created/labeled before this boot's SELinux policy load at
# t=4.697s -- "systemd[1]: Successfully loaded SELinux policy" fires at the
# exact same timestamp -- and thus stamped with the kernel's initial SID,
# which never gets retroactively relabeled).
#
# Narrow, targeted fix per this investigation's established discipline:
# grant exactly the two denied permissions, exactly for the observed
# scontext/tclass/tcontext combination. Not yet empirically confirmed to
# resolve (or contribute to resolving) the boot hang -- staged here for the
# next mls-hang-live-ssh-real.fmf round, same pattern as the two other
# known-insufficient-alone fixes already staged in mls-udev-ptrace-fix/ and
# mls-hang-capture/{inittransition,udevnoatsecure}.te.
#
# ROUND 3 (branch mls-hang-nsfs-fix-round3, built on mls-hang-nsfs-fix-
# round2 commit cfa3f10b): two independent Testing Farm runs of round 2's
# `{ read write }`-only rule (requests 74241c27-de78-47c3-9044-
# 91b93a1800d0 and 382518d7-f6d5-4a30-b500-bb86262dfb99) both hit a NEW,
# third denial on this exact object -- granting read/write let the
# original denial's syscall through, but exposed the next one right
# behind it, the same "peel the onion" pattern round 2 itself already
# found for nsfs_t:
#
#   audit[576]: AVC avc:  denied  { getattr } for  pid=576
#     comm="systemd-udevd" path="socket:[1803]" dev="sockfs" ino=1803
#     scontext=system_u:system_r:udev_t:s0-s15:c0.c1023
#     tcontext=system_u:system_r:kernel_t:s15:c0.c1023
#     tclass=netlink_kobject_uevent_socket permissive=0
#
# (PID/inode differ slightly between the two runs -- pid=576/ino=1803 in
# the first, pid=581/ino=976 in the second -- confirming this is a
# reproducible per-boot check, not a one-off.) Unlike round 2's
# fatal-crash-loop denials, this one is NON-FATAL: systemd-udevd logs
# "Failed to verify socket type of 5, ignoring: Permission denied" and
# continues running normally. Fixed here anyway, on this investigation's
# same "close every real denial we can see" discipline, since a
# still-enforcing gap left in place is still an unresolved data point.
#
# Round 3's own first Testing Farm confirmation of the { read write
# getattr } rule above (requests ed7f40c9-dfef-4a92-920f-a95fc24eecfa and
# 6a891543-231d-412e-a5bd-195fd4243737 -- both a MAJOR WIN, the enforcing
# hang itself is fully resolved, ens5/lo both get real addresses, no
# tmt-reboot timeout) found a THIRD layer on this exact object, same
# onion-peeling pattern as nsfs_t/tmpfs_t before it -- ioctl(SIOCGSKNS,
# 0x894c) itself, the same socket-netns-identification ioctl already
# documented in udevnsfs.te's own history, this time invoked directly on
# the kernel_t-labeled uevent socket rather than udev_t's own netns file:
#
#   audit[644]: AVC avc:  denied  { ioctl } for  pid=644
#     comm="systemd-udevd" path="socket:[1904]" dev="sockfs" ino=1904
#     ioctlcmd=0x894c scontext=system_u:system_r:udev_t:s0-s15:c0.c1023
#     tcontext=system_u:system_r:kernel_t:s15:c0.c1023
#     tclass=netlink_kobject_uevent_socket permissive=0
#
# (confirmed identical, only pid/inode differ, in both runs). Also
# non-fatal -- part of the same "Failed to verify socket type ... ignoring"
# code path -- but closed here for completeness, so a future run's AVC
# sweep on this object is (hopefully) finally empty.
gen_require(`
	type udev_t, kernel_t;
')

allow udev_t kernel_t:netlink_kobject_uevent_socket { read write getattr ioctl };
